Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

Hi Dave,

Thanks for the series; the bring-up ordering fixes and the "don't fail
the probe on a down link" change both look like real improvements over
the current driver. Went through all 9 patches against the actual
diffs; comments inline per patch.

Patch 1/9 - power the slot from vpcie3v3-supply:

    +=09ret =3D spacemit_pcie_get_vpcie3v3(dev, port, &pci->vpcie3v3);
    +=09if (ret) {
    +=09=09dev_warn(dev, "vpcie3v3 supply not available: %d\n", ret);
    +=09=09pci->vpcie3v3 =3D NULL;
    +=09}
    ...
    +=09if (pci->vpcie3v3) {
    +=09=09ret =3D regulator_set_enable_if_allowed(pci->vpcie3v3, true);
    +=09=09if (ret) {
    +=09=09=09dev_err(dev, "failed to enable vpcie3v3 supply: %d\n", ret);
    +=09=09=09return ret;
    +=09=09}
    +=09}

Lookup failures and enable failures are handled with deliberately
different severities (warn-and-continue vs. fail probe), which matches
the commit message's reasoning about always-on fixed regulators without
a driver built in. That asymmetry is fine. One thing to check: once
vpcie3v3 is enabled, nothing later in pcie_dw_spacemit_probe() disables
it again if a later step fails (generic_phy_init(), pcie_dw_setup_host(),
the iATU setup in 4/9). Not a new problem introduced by this patch, but
worth deciding whether probe failure after this point should leave the
slot powered or not.

Patch 2/9 - hold the PHY interface in reset until link start:

    -=09=09reg &=3D ~(u32)APP_HOLD_PHY_RST;
    -=09=09spacemit_pcie_writel(pci, PCIECTRL_K1X_CONF_DEVICE_CMD, reg);

Straightforward removal of the premature deassert, matching Linux's and
FreeBSD's sequencing. No issues.

Patch 3/9 - soft reset the controller before bring-up:

    +=09reg =3D spacemit_pcie_readl(pci, PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC);
    +=09spacemit_pcie_writel(pci, PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC, reg | PCIE_SOFT_RESET);
    +=09spacemit_pcie_readl(pci, PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC);
    +=09mdelay(2);
    +=09spacemit_pcie_writel(pci, PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC, reg & ~PCIE_SOFT_RESET);

spacemit_pcie_toggle_soft_reset() is called before
clk_enable_bulk(&pci->clks) / reset_deassert_bulk(&pci->rsts). Since
PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC lives in the APMU control logic block rather than the
DBI register space, that is presumably fine, but it would help to say
in the commit message (or a comment) that this register is reachable
independent of the pcie clocks/resets this driver otherwise gates
register access on - a reviewer can't tell that from the diff alone.
Read-modify-write using the cached `reg` for the final clear is correct
here since nothing else touches PCIE_CTRL_LOGIC in between.

Patch 4/9 - check clock, reset and iATU setup results:

    +=09ret =3D reset_deassert_bulk(&pci->rsts);
    +=09if (ret) {
    +=09=09dev_err(dev, "failed to deassert resets: %d\n", ret);
    +=09=09clk_disable_bulk(&pci->clks);
    +=09=09return ret;
    +=09}
    ...
    +=09if (ret)
    +=09=09return -EIO;

Good catch overall, and the clock/reset pair correctly unwinds
clk_enable_bulk() when reset_deassert_bulk() fails. But the new
iATU check at the end just returns -EIO without disabling clocks or
re-asserting resets, unlike the error path two hunks above in the same
function - probe error handling ends up inconsistent within one
function. Given 1/9 leaves vpcie3v3 enabled on later failures too, this
driver doesn't really do unwind-on-error anywhere past this point; if
that's accepted as out of scope, it's at least worth a one-line note in
the commit message.

Patch 5/9 - report link up from the link status register:

    -=09val =3D readl(pci->dw.dbi_base + PCIE_PORT_DEBUG0);
    -=09val &=3D PORT_LOGIC_LTSSM_STATE_MASK;
    +=09val =3D spacemit_pcie_phy_ahb_readl(pci, K1X_PHY_AHB_LINK_STS);
    +=09return (val & SMLH_LINK_UP) && (val & RDLH_LINK_UP);

Matches Linux/FreeBSD exactly (SMLH + RDLH), and cleanly drops the now
dead PORT_LOGIC_LTSSM_STATE_* defines from the header. No issues.

Patch 6/9 - do not force the link to 2.5 GT/s:

    +=09if (speed) {
    +=09=09lnkcap =3D readl(cap + PCI_EXP_LNKCAP);
    +=09=09lnkcap &=3D ~PCI_EXP_LNKCAP_SLS;
    +=09=09lnkcap |=3D speed;
    ...
    +=09pcie->max_link_speed =3D dev_read_u32_default(dev, "max-link-speed", 0);
    +=09if (pcie->max_link_speed > LINK_SPEED_GEN_4) {

Finding the registers through pcie_dw_find_capability() instead of the
hardcoded 0x7c/0xa0 offsets is the right fix, and gating the write on
"speed" being nonzero correctly preserves the hardware default when
max-link-speed isn't set. The upper-bound check against
LINK_SPEED_GEN_4 is reasonable; no issues.

Patch 7/9 - do not advertise ASPM L1:

    +=09clrbits_le32(cap + PCI_EXP_LNKCAP, PCI_EXP_LNKCAP_ASPM_L1);

This is applied unconditionally to every K1 port, for every board,
citing NVMe drives that error out with L1 enabled. Linux's driver does
the same unconditionally, so this matches upstream, but it does mean
boards/drives that are fine with L1 (and would benefit from it) lose
that option with no way to opt back in via the device tree. Given this
is a direct port of a known Linux workaround I'm fine with it as-is,
but flagging it in case there's an appetite for gating it on a
DT quirk property instead of hardcoding it for all K1 ports.

Patch 8/9 - set the link width from num-lanes:

    +=09pcie->num_lanes =3D dev_read_u32_default(dev, "num-lanes", 0);
    +=09dw_pcie_link_set_max_link_width(&pci->dw, pci->num_lanes);

Unlike max-link-speed in 6/9, there's no validation here: a bogus
num-lanes value in the device tree (anything other than 1/2/4/8/16, or
a typo like 3) is passed straight to
dw_pcie_link_set_max_link_width() with no bounds check before it
reaches the hardware. Worth adding the same kind of sanity check 6/9
added for max-link-speed, or confirming dw_pcie_link_set_max_link_width()
already validates/clamps internally.

Patch 9/9 - do not fail the probe when the link is down:

    +=09if (!pcie_dw_spacemit_pcie_link_up(pci))
    +=09=09printf("PCIE-%d: Link down\n", dev_seq(dev));
    +=09else
    +=09=09printf("PCIE-%d: Link up (Gen%d-x%d, Bus%d)\n", ...);
    ...
    +=09if (PCI_BUS(bdf) !=3D pci->dw.first_busno && !is_link_up(pci)) {
    +=09=09*valuep =3D pci_get_ff(size);
    +=09=09return 0;
    +=09}

Matches dw_pcie_other_conf_map_bus() semantics and avoids the repeated
one-second re-probe penalty described in the commit message - good
change. The one concern: probe() now always returns 0 whether the slot
is empty or whether bring-up silently failed for a real reason (bad
PERST# timing, misconfigured clocks that still happen to "enable"
without an actual functional link, etc). Right now both cases produce
the same "Link down" printf() and the same successful probe, so a real
misconfiguration on a populated slot looks identical to an empty slot
in the log. Consider dev_warn()/log_warning() instead of (or in
addition to) printf() so a down link is visible through normal U-Boot
log level filtering and distinguishable from "I expected this slot to
be empty."

Verdict:

None of these 9 patches carry any Reviewed-by, Acked-by, or Tested-by
tags yet. The series structure (one logical fix per patch, in bring-up
order) is good and I wouldn't ask for it to be split or squashed
differently. The only patch I'd really want addressed before a v2 is
8/9 (missing num-lanes validation), with 4/9's inconsistent iATU error
unwind and 9/9's silent-masking concern as secondary asks; 7/9's
unconditional ASPM-L1 removal is a question rather than a blocker.

Dave - can you send a v2 with at least the num-lanes validation added,
and reply with your thinking on the iATU unwind and the down-link
logging? Happy to test again once that's in.

Thanks,
Leo
